@@ -4164,7 +4164,7 @@ entries:
     date: '1966-05-18'
     thread: china-leadership
     c: 'Zhou Enlai reports to Mao on strengthening the Peking garrison and the guard of key offices in the capital: Mao, "do as proposed" (mid-May, no day). Also mid-May, Mao reads Lin Biao''s May 17 report of a General Political Department paper: Lin''s directive of May 11, that appointments of the first and second leaders of the large military regions, services and three headquarters go to Mao and the Center before they are announced, a system to last; Mao: "return to Lin."'
-    n: '[[w.zedong-mao-zedong-nianpu]], V, May, mid, 1966 (the two entries "5月中旬" and "同旬"); [[w.macfarquhar-origins-of-the-cultural]], III, 459 (Mao has Zhou tighten security in Peking and the Zhongnanhai); [[w.jian-zhou-enlai]], 541 (Zhou, on Mao''s orders, deploys more "reliable military units" for the "safety of Beijing and the surrounding areas," and knows he must stay in Peking; not the day); [[w.pu-weihua-zalan-jiu-shijie]], 113 (mid-May 1966, during the enlarged Politburo meeting, Mao raises the strengthening of the capital''s guard; he dares not lightly come back to Peking). Made public: 1967 ([[w.zhongyang-shouzhang-jianghua-1967]], Zhou''s account to Red Guards of Mao''s orders). To check: Zhou Enlai, speech to Red Guards on Mao''s security orders, in [[w.zhongyang-shouzhang-jianghua-1967]], 1, 249 (cited by [[w.macfarquhar-origins-of-the-cultural]], III, 459 n.160b); Michael Schoenhals, communication to the author (cited by [[w.macfarquhar-origins-of-the-cultural]], III, 459 n.160c); [[w.zhou-enlai-junshi-huodong]], 628–629 (cited by [[w.jian-zhou-enlai]], 541 n.87a); [[w.zhou-enlai-nianpu-1949]], III, 31–32 (cited by [[w.jian-zhou-enlai]], 541 n.87b). Names: Zhou Enlai, Mao Zedong, Lin Biao. Check: the nianpu dates the two papers only to the middle ten days of May; May 18 is Zhou''s report''s date. Check: whether [[w.zhongyang-shouzhang-jianghua-1967]] (1967), a Red Guard compilation, on which the year rests, was printed for internal circulation; if so, Made public is the year of the next work cited.'
